        return None

lessons.solution:
import asyncio
class SingleFlight:
    def __init__(self): self.tasks={}
    async def get(self,key,factory):
        task=self.tasks.get(key)
        if task is None or task.done():
            task=asyncio.create_task(factory(key));self.tasks[key]=task
            def clear(done):
                if self.tasks.get(key) is done:self.tasks.pop(key,None)
                if not done.cancelled():done.exception()
            task.add_done_callback(clear)
        return await asyncio.shield(task)

def error_leaves(error):
    if isinstance(error,BaseExceptionGroup):
        return [name for child in error.exceptions for name in error_leaves(child)]
    return [type(error).__name__]

lessons.body:
# Enforce an invariant through a data descriptor

Attribute lookup has an order. A data descriptor defines setting as well as getting, so it can enforce a rule before an instance dictionary value is considered. A read-only non-data descriptor has different precedence and can be shadowed.

Use a per-instance storage name established by __set_name__. Storing the value on the descriptor would share it among every instance of the owning class. The exercise checks independent owners, class access and validation of booleans, because bool is an int subclass and often slips through a domain-level integer check.

```python
class Owner:
    def __init__(self):
        self._value=2
    @property
    def value(self):return self._value
    @value.setter
    def value(self,v):self._value=max(0,v)
x=Owner();x.value=-1
print(x.value)
```

## Review the design

Defining __set__ makes this a data descriptor. Its precedence protects ordinary assignment paths, but direct modification of backing storage remains possible; Python attribute conventions are not a security boundary.

